key-list-ordering: sorted, so that two runs enumerate one corpus
  identically.  Directory order is filesystem-dependent and would differ
  between two hosts for no behavioural reason.
engine-initialisation-happens-at: deployment, before any request in this
  flow is sent, so its evidence is a startup-log region rather than a
  response body
engine-initialisation-evidence: startup/workflow-engine-init.log
engine-initialisation-lines-matched: 50
startup-evidence-directory-note: the region is written under startup/ and
  never under a directory named after a log folder, because that name is
  ignored by the repository at any depth and the evidence would be
  silently uncommitted while every local check still passed
startup-timing-caveat: the repository deployment step documents that the
  first startup takes several minutes, and the engine deploys its
  definitions during that window.  A definitions figure read from a running
  engine before deployment finishes therefore measures timing rather than
  behaviour, and an engine-side count must be read only afterwards.  The
  figures above are corpus measurements taken from the tree and are not
  subject to that race at all, which is why they can be recorded here even
  when no engine is reachable.
startup-timing-caveat-source: the deployment step in README.md, which also
  names the container log to watch

===== workflow-instantiation-observations =====
comparison-criterion: identical process instantiation and task assignment
residual-risk-status: OBSERVED HERE, NOT CLEARED.  The process engine is
  the oldest load-bearing component in the reactor, its version is
  unchanged, and it could not be bootstrapped for testing without a
  database.  It was assigned to this gate rather than declared safe.
  Nothing in this capture may be read as verifying it.
observed-process-definition-key: not-exposed-by-the-workflow-start-endpoint: it answers with the complaint, not with a process-instance descriptor
observed-process-definition-identifier: not-exposed-by-the-workflow-start-endpoint: it answers with the complaint, not with a process-instance descriptor
observed-process-instance-identifier: not-exposed-by-the-workflow-start-endpoint: it answers with the complaint, not with a process-instance descriptor
observed-execution-identifier: not-exposed-by-the-workflow-start-endpoint: it answers with the complaint, not with a process-instance descriptor
observed-deployment-identifier: not-exposed-by-the-workflow-start-endpoint: it answers with the complaint, not with a process-instance descriptor
observed-instance-state: not-observed
observed-task-identifier: (not observed - no process instance was created by this run)
observed-task-definition-key: not-observed|none
observed-task-name: not-observed|none
observed-task-assignee: not-observed|none
observed-task-candidate-group: not-observed|none
observed-process-variables: (not observed - no process instance was created by this run)
observed-task-count-for-this-run-object: 0
0
assignee-is-preserved-not-redacted: an assignee is a user identity and it
  is the behaviour under test, because task assignment is half of this
  criterion.  It is not a credential and is never replaced.
declared-process-definition-key: cmComplaintWorkflow
declared-process-definition-key-found-in-corpus: yes
declared-process-definition-key-found-in-corpus-basis: the corpus is
  the process definitions this repository ships, so this row establishes
  that the definition this flow is named for EXISTS.  It is deliberately
  not evidence that the engine ran it — that is what the loaded-definitions
  count from the startup region is for, and the two are asserted separately
  because they fail for different reasons.
declared-process-name: Complaint review and approval process
declared-task-definition-key: approveComplaint
declared-task-name-expression: Approve Complaint '${complaintTitle}'
declared-task-assignee-expression: ${currentApprover}
declared-task-candidate-group: (none declared; this definition assigns directly)
declared-assignee-collection-variable: approvers
declared-assignee-element-variable: currentApprover
declared-task-due-date: P3D
declared-task-priority: 50
declared-process-variables-the-definition-references: approvers, currentApprover, and the title the task name interpolates
declared-read-from: <REPO-ROOT>/acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-complaint-plugin/src/main/resources/activiti/DefaultComplaintWorkflow_v5.bpmn20.xml
normalisation-applied-to-this-section: the engine-assigned process-instance,
  execution, task and deployment identifiers, and the version and sequence
  components of the version-suffixed process-definition identifier.  Each
  comes from a database sequence and differs on every run.
normalisation-deliberately-not-applied: the process definition KEY, the task
  definition key, the task name, the assignee or candidate group, the
  instance state, the process variables and the definitions count.  Those
  are the values this criterion compares; normalising any of them would
  leave two captures agreeing on placeholders and demonstrating nothing.
key-versus-identifier: the definition KEY is stable and behaviour-bearing;
  the definition IDENTIFIER is the key plus an engine-assigned version and
  sequence number and is volatile.  The substitution keeps the key and
  replaces only the two numeric components, so the distinction stays
  visible in the evidence.

